A||||10||84_duke||Converting 84 to GT dual exhaust||||1||
Z||000000||84_duke||12-04-2001||02:36 PM||||I would like to convert my 84 to the GT style dual exhaust (only one muffler still, right?)  So, can I pick up, say the ocelot performance exhaust from the fiero store, or is there something better out there for the duke?  Will a stock GT setup off of a V6 fit my car? <BR>Also, what model rear bumber should I look for to accomodate the right side exhaust tips?<BR>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>84_duke||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tigger||12-04-2001||02:54 PM||||Stock exhaust will work.  An exhaust shop can splice it in by welding in a short spacer where the exhausts runs under the engine back.  I had to make a hanger bracket for the right side.  The holes are already stamped in the cradle and it's a simple bolt on if you can get the hanger and bolts from the V6 cradle.<P>I believe the early coupe (black bumper pad) fascia won't work or will have to be cut and modified.  Aero GT and formula/88 coupe bumpers should be no problem.||||reg||1
Z||000002||84_duke||12-04-2001||03:00 PM||||Sounds fairly straight forward.<BR>thanks for the info Tigger!<P>------------------<BR>84_duke||||reg||14
Z||000003||Tigger||12-04-2001||03:33 PM||||No prob, sorry I don't have any info on what aftermarket exhaust works best with the 4 cylinder.  I question if going aftermarket would result in all that much more in performance over the stock duals.<P>You might be able find something searching the Technical Discussion area to dig up some info on exhausts in past threads.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 12-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||sprnt96||12-04-2001||06:03 PM||||I did the very same thing with my 84 SE. I had a stock v-6 dual exhaust from the fiero store installed, just had a cat eliminator pipe put on and megaphone tops.  I bought hangers from Rodney for the back...You would never know by the looks of it that it is a 4 banger!||||reg||1
Z||000005||sprnt96||12-04-2001||06:04 PM||||I forgot to mention I did put on GT front and rear facias tho..||||reg||1
Z||000006||MR2KLR||12-05-2001||07:18 PM||||My 88 coupe has a OEM dual exhaust from a GT with OEM GT tips. Looks good, it dosen't sound to bad either I need to ditch the cat though.<P>------------------<BR>  <BR>Black 88 Coupe: 120 MPH spedo, Aux. gauge pod, 195/55VR14 Potenza RE-71 tires, GT exhaust & ground F/X Soon to have 16" White Beretta GTZ W/ 215/45ZR16's<BR><A HREF="http://community-2.webtv.net/TOMTOM81/MYNEWTOY/" TARGET=_blank>Tom's Page</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Jncomutt||12-05-2001||08:02 PM||||I Just Did that..<BR>I posted a topic about how to get sound.. You'll love the sound... as soon as I get it recorded.  I have pics and such.  I did it different tho.  I used a Y pipe right after the cat. and ran 2 pipes back, its nice!  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Jncomutt||12-05-2001||09:35 PM||||I forgot to mention.  I used a rear GT bumper from an 85 due to the Ribbed molding.  It looks great.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Banzai||12-06-2001||07:10 AM||||I put the stock V6 replacement from the Fiero Store on my 84'.  I used a pipe to elimnate the cat and bolted $1.99 hangers to the cradle mounting points. The tips both have 1/2" of clearance under the rear fascia. It looks good and sound killer for a duke. Now I wish I would have spent the extra $40 for the Ocelot system. The Ocelot has compression bends instead of mandrel bends but they are better than the "wrinkle" bends on the stock replacement. Not  a huge issue with the Duke, but when I go 3800 it might prove restrictive.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroRacer||12-06-2001||04:17 PM||||I ran into an Indy today that had the quad tips.  That thing sounded Killer for a duke!!  That is, until he speeded off leaving me in the dust as I walked home.||||reg||1
